From: dresden196 <50474030+dresden196@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] NickelOS: picture previews on the desktop and in Files
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Files already shows previews of the pictures and videos Chrome can decode
(its image loader extension: JPEG, PNG, GIF, WebP, AVIF, RAW photos, PDFs,
H.264/VP9/AV1 video). What it can't decode got a plain icon: HEIC/HEIF
photos (what phones take) and video in codecs it has no decoder for (H.265
without a hardware decoder). The desktop's icons (0057) had no previews
at all.

- Files: HEIC/HEIF are pictures (file_types.json5). Its image loader asks
  the system's thumbnailers for them, and for a video it can't play:
  imageLoaderPrivate.getNickelThumbnail(url, width, height), which asks
  nickel-appd (org.nickelos.Apps.Thumbnail(path, size) -> PNG; sandboxed
  libheif and ffmpegthumbnailer, cached in ~/.cache/thumbnails under the
  path Linux apps know the file by).
- ash::ThumbnailLoader takes HEIC/HEIF, and can keep a picture's
  proportions (ThumbnailRequest's crop false: the image loader's
  maxWidth/maxHeight) instead of cutting it to a square.
- The desktop: NickelDesktopClient::LoadThumbnail, through that loader;
  pictures, RAW photos, videos and PDFs show their preview in place of
  the tile, in proportion, with rounded corners, a thin edge and a play
  badge on videos.
- The image loader is version 0.6 with its service worker renamed
  (service_worker_20261012.js): a profile keeps the old worker script
  otherwise (Chrome's service worker script cache), which has no
  getNickelThumbnail; the old one reloads the extension on a version it
  doesn't know (Chromium's own mechanism, the § in its sources).

